expect:
Pass borrowed resources per call, or keep them only in the owner. Show in the diff where any unavoidable new holder is released on disconnect or teardown. Make exclusive ownership an explicit precondition, and guarantee it at every caller. Route cross-submodule calls through a context method that dispatches to the owner. Carry new state on the existing pipeline as a new event or message type that the owner consumes, and keep transport handlers to forwarding only. Delete the duplicate path. Check: Name the owner of each tensor, block, submodule or piece of state the diff touches. After the diff, count:
- how many objects retain it, and which teardown path releases each one;
- who can write to it, and whether another request or component can observe an in-place write;
- whether any sibling holds a direct reference to another sibling;
- how many mechanisms carry the same kind of state.
Any count above one, or a holder with no shown release, is a finding.

Add a feature-detecting compatibility helper, or remove or sync the fallback, at every call site and in every version variant. On recovery, re-run the full handshake and verify every renegotiated value against the cached one before re-registering resources or marking the connection healthy, and handle mismatches explicitly. Choose behavior explicitly for each transfer context. In the PR, state the upstream semantics relied on and how the observed behavior deviates, and justify the LMCache-side fix or link an upstream issue or PR. Check: List every counterpart this code can run against or be invoked from:
- vendored, fallback or older copies, and each version-specific variant;
- a peer restarted with different defaults;
- LMCache-driven vs engine-driven transfer contexts;
- the upstream engine's expected semantics.
For each one, check that every API called exists, every negotiated value is re-verified, and every assumed behavior is written down. If the change compensates for upstream behavior, check whether the PR says why the fix belongs in LMCache.
